  "prompt": "Feature: an optional duration on each recipe instruction step.\n\nUsers want to record how long a step takes (\"rest 1 hour\"), see it on the step and edit it in the recipe editor. Store it per step, in whole seconds. The editor and step view wiring is not graded.\n\nExpected:\n1. RecipeStep gets duration_seconds (durationSeconds in JSON): optional, default null, a whole number of seconds from 1 to 2147483647. Missing, null, 0 and 0.0 all mean no duration (None). Whole numbers in range are accepted, including JSON numbers written as floats with no fraction (1.0, 1e0, 2147483647.0); they are stored and returned as int. Anything else is a validation error: true and false, negatives (-1), fractions (0.5, 1.5, 2147483647.5), inf, -inf and nan, any string (\"90\", \"0\", \"PT1M\", \"\", \" \"), and values above 2147483647 (2147483648, 2147483648.0, 1e30). Nothing is rounded or read from a string. In RecipeStep.model_json_schema(by_alias=True), durationSeconds is not required, its default is null, and its anyOf has exactly two entries: an integer with minimum 1 and maximum 2147483647 (no other keywords), then null.\n2. The RecipeInstruction ORM model gets a duration_seconds column on recipe_instructions: Integer, nullable, with no default and no server default. A RecipeInstruction built from a step's model_dump() keeps the exact seconds, and handle_one_to_many_list can set (42) and clear (None) it on an existing instruction without touching its text.\n3. The migration only adds that column. Its offline SQL for sqlite and postgresql contains ALTER TABLE recipe_instructions ADD COLUMN duration_seconds INTEGER, with no NOT NULL, no DEFAULT and no UPDATE; existing rows get NULL. Its downgrade drops only that column and leaves every other table and row as it was: on a populated database, downgrading, upgrading, setting values, downgrading again and upgrading again must work repeatedly, and after an upgrade no row has a duration.\n4. Through the API, PUT /api/recipes/{slug} stores each step's durationSeconds and GET returns it (null for a step sent without it or with null). A later PUT can change it, or clear it with null or 0, and the step keeps its text. An invalid value answers 422 and nothing is saved. The raw JSON export (TemplateService(...).render(recipe, \"raw\")) includes durationSeconds, and importing that JSON in a zip (POST /api/recipes/create/zip) restores it.\n\nMigration identity: revision id afdc42353aa4, revising a91720b8a7e7 (it becomes the only head), message \"add duration to recipe steps\", created 2026-10-21 00:00:00. Its file is named from those values by the project's migration template, 2026-10-21-00.00.00_afdc42353aa4_add_duration_to_recipe_steps.py; the tests load it by that path and check its down_revision.\n\nThis change needs a schema migration (an Alembic revision under the project's migrations) that can be rolled back: its downgrade must restore the previous schema, and the migration must not drop columns or tables or run destructive SQL against existing data. Upgrading, downgrading one step and upgrading again must leave the schema as it was. Chain the revision onto the current head (it becomes the only head) with the project's date-prefixed file name, which must sort after every existing migration: the tests derive the latest revision from the file order.\n\nTests: `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests/unit_tests tests/integration_tests/user_recipe_tests` for quick loops; the whole backend suite is `python -m pytest -q -p no:cacheprovider tests` (about 8 minutes).",
